<Base
  title="LCI·GRAPH — Alissa Nicole"
  description="Case study: LCI·GRAPH, an open-source pipeline that extracts life-cycle inventory data from academic papers into a queryable database."
  current="home"
>
  <p class="backlink"><a href="/">← All projects</a></p>
  <h1>LCI·GRAPH</h1>
  <p class="tagline">An open-source pipeline that turns the LCA literature into a queryable inventory database.</p>

  <h2>The problem</h2>
  <p>
    Some of the best life-cycle inventory data in the field is published in academic
    papers — and then effectively buried. The numbers live in PDF tables and
    supplementary files, in whatever format the authors happened to use. If you need
    them, you re-transcribe them by hand; if you want to compare across studies, you
    first have to undo every paper's idiosyncrasies. Practitioners redo this work
    over and over, privately, and throw it away.
  </p>

  <h2>What it does</h2>
  <p>
    LCI·GRAPH ingests a paper and its supplements, extracts the inventory tables,
    links every flow to a canonical dataset, and stores the result in a database you
    can actually query — then exports to the formats practitioners already use
    (openLCA JSON-LD, SimaPro CSV, ILCD, EcoSpold2).
  </p>

  <figure>
    <img src="/snapshots/lci-graph/studies-browser.png" alt="LCI·GRAPH study browser: a list of ingested papers with DOI, system boundary, background database, and review status." loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption>Papers in the database — each with its DOI, system boundary, and background database preserved.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Part 1 — the extraction pipeline</h2>
  <p>
    The pipeline's job is not just to find the numbers in a paper — it's to keep
    everything that makes those numbers meaningful. On the left: a table buried on page
    S12 of a supplementary PDF. On the right: the same inventory as typed, unit-checked
    rows in the database, every value traceable back to that table.
  </p>

  <h3>From paper to database</h3>
  <figure>
    <img src="/snapshots/lci-graph/paper-table-s6.png" alt="Table S6 from the paper's supplementary PDF: LCI data of CSTR 10 bar using serpentine, with columns for electricity, thermal energy, and carbon footprint per life-cycle stage." loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption>
      Before: Table S6 in the supplementary PDF of <a href="https://doi.org/10.1039/D0SE00190B">Ostovari et al. 2020</a> — electricity,
      thermal energy, and footprint per life-cycle stage, with footnoted assumptions.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure>
    <img src="/snapshots/lci-graph/extracted-cstr10bar.png" alt="The same table after extraction into LCI·GRAPH: typed flows with amounts and units, methodology context, export buttons, and data-quality warnings." loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption>
      After: the same table in LCI·GRAPH. The 20 / 324 / 441 kWh rows are all there —
      and where the paper is ambiguous (an empty thermal-energy cell), the pipeline
      raises an explicit data-quality warning instead of silently guessing. The
      footnotes survived too, as assumptions on the dataset.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>What has to survive extraction</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Amounts are data, not text to clean up.</strong> Every amount is stored as a
    verbatim string, so scientific notation survives — and an explicit zero ("PPS
    granulate — 0 g/kg") is kept as an author claim (<em>we looked; it's nothing</em>),
    not dropped as a missing value.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The reference flow is the denominator everything hangs on.</strong> Each
    process is stored normalized to one unit of its declared reference flow ("per kg of
    REM alloy"). When processes chain together, upstream inventories are rescaled by the
    exchange ratio at read time — the scaled version is never stored, because that's
    where conversion errors would live.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>An inventory without its boundary conditions is not data, it's decoration.</strong> Every extracted dataset carries its methodology context — system boundary, LCIA
    method, allocation, source DOI — displayed next to the numbers wherever they
    appear. Two inventories are only comparable when you know what each one counted.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img src="/snapshots/lci-graph/lci-inventory.png" alt="An extracted unit process: PEM electrolysis stack construction, with methodology context and a flow table mapped to ecoinvent datasets." loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption>An extracted unit process: flows mapped to canonical ecoinvent datasets, methodology context pinned to the top.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>The stack: LLMs draft, rules decide, humans approve</h3>
  <figure>
    <img src="/snapshots/lci-graph/pipeline-stack.svg" alt="Pipeline diagram: input paper and supplements flow through a deterministic routing gate into multi-pass LLM extraction, then deterministic and LLM validation, then human review, then SQLite, then one-click exports." loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption>
      The pipeline, end to end. Blue is where the model runs, grey is deterministic code,
      orange is the human. The model only ever drafts — the transitions between stages
      are all rules.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    <strong>Exactly one of three extraction paths runs per paper, and routing is a file
    check, not a model call.</strong> Papers with supplements go supplement-first: every
    document is parsed to text with a table registry (pdfplumber, python-docx, openpyxl),
    a first pass reads the main paper for metadata and boundary context only, and a
    second extracts the LCI tables in one structured call. PDF-only papers go vision:
    the model reads the PDF pages directly — the corpus's gold standard, at 3–5× the
    cost. A plain text path is the last-resort fallback.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Nothing the model says enters the database as fact.</strong> Extractions land
    as <em>pending review</em>: deterministic checks run first (schema, inter-process
    links, variable amounts resolved against the table registry, ghost processes
    dropped), then a final LLM pass re-sends every table's JSON with its source table
    and asks "does this match?" Corrections are made in a review interface — and those
    corrections feed back as extraction rules, so the same mistake is not made twice.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Accuracy is measured, not asserted.</strong> The pipeline is evaluated
    against a golden set of hand-verified extractions, so a prompt change or model
    swap shows up as a number, not a vibe.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img src="/snapshots/lci-graph/meta-analysis.png" alt="Meta-analysis view: canonical materials ranked by number of flows and studies they appear in." loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption>Once flows are normalized to canonical materials, cross-study questions become answerable — e.g. which materials the literature actually covers.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>Export to the tools you already use</h3>
  <p>
    A database nobody can get data out of is a roach motel. Every study and every
    individual process exports with one click to the formats practitioners actually
    work in: <strong>openLCA JSON-LD</strong>, <strong>SimaPro CSV</strong>, <strong>ILCD</strong> (GaBi),
    and <strong>EcoSpold2</strong>. The openLCA export
    is a standards-compliant JSON-LD package — processes, flows, units, and flow
    properties as linked entities:
  </p>
  <pre>{jsonldSnippet}</pre>
  <p>
    In openLCA it's <em>File → Import → JSON-LD</em>, and the process drops into the
    navigation tree ready to build a product system from. No re-typing, no unit
    conversion errors, no lost footnotes. Here's the whole round trip:
  </p>
  <figure>
    <video src="/snapshots/lci-graph/export-openlca.mp4" autoplay muted loop playsinline></video>
    <figcaption>
      Export side: from the study page to a downloaded openLCA JSON-LD package in one click.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Part 2 — the graph layer, put to a falsifiable test</h2>
  <p>
    The relational store works, but the data underneath it is graph-shaped: activities
    connected by flows, with identity ("what substance is this?"), provenance ("which
    paper says so?"), and hierarchy ("this part belongs to that product"). Every new
    comparison feature kept dying on the relational shape. Still, "use a graph database"
    is also a fashionable answer — so instead of migrating on faith, I framed it as a
    falsifiable hypothesis and built a proof of concept that was designed to be able to fail.
  </p>
  <p>
    The hypothesis: a labeled-property-graph vocabulary of five node labels
    (Activity, Exchange, Material, Dataset, Source) and eight edge types can hold both
    paper-extracted inventories <em>and</em> an industrial bill of materials without schema
    additions, and turns the comparison and composition questions into simple traversals
    that are painful over the relational equivalent. Four sub-claims made it falsifiable:
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>H1 — the vocabulary is sufficient.</strong> Both worlds represented
    faithfully, or a sixth node label / ninth edge type has to appear.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>H2 — identity is one problem.</strong> Cross-paper matching ("same
    electricity, three papers") and intra-study linkage are both missing edges of the
    same type, closable by one engine — falsified if either world needs special-casing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>H3 — roll-up works.</strong> A study's internal flows cancel and its boundary
    exchanges sum to a correct black-box view, with chain math computed at read time —
    falsified by disagreeing with a hand-calculated roll-up.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>H4 — the graph pulls its weight.</strong> The test questions take materially
    less effort than their SQL equivalents — falsified, honestly, if they don't.
  </p>
  <h3>The vocabulary</h3>
  <p>
    The heart of the vocabulary is bipartite — <strong>Activities</strong> (things that
    happen) and <strong>Exchanges</strong> (quantified stuff crossing their boundary).
    Exchange is a first-class node for a structural reason: in a property graph, edges
    can't have edges — and provenance, dataset mapping, and canonical identity all need
    something to attach to. Around that heart: a <strong>Material</strong> library for
    identity, <strong>Dataset</strong> for background references, and <strong>Source</strong> so
    every node knows where it came from.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img src="/snapshots/lci-graph/graph-schema.svg" alt="Graph schema diagram: Activity and Exchange as the bipartite heart, connected by PRODUCES, CONSUMES, and FEEDS edges; Exchange points to Material via INSTANCE_OF, to Dataset via FROM_DATASET, and everything points to Source via SOURCED_FROM." loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption>
      The v0 vocabulary. Grounded in two published approaches (LCIKG, GraphDLCA) and one
      decision that shaped everything: Exchange-as-node.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>A real paper in the vocabulary</h3>
  <p>
    This is an actual extraction redrawn in the vocabulary — a study on NdFeB magnet
    recycling, mapped from the live database. Two design surfaces show up immediately.
    The provider's output and the consumer's input are the same stuff under different
    strings ("milled magnet powder (ca 200 µm)" vs "cleaned, demagnetized, ball-milled
    EOL magnet") — so intra-study linkage and cross-paper identity are one matching
    problem, not two. And the 6.45 kg on the chain link is where the denominators
    change: upstream amounts are per kg of powder, downstream per kg of alloy, so
    roll-up math has to multiply through the conversion at read time.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img src="/snapshots/lci-graph/magnet-mapping.svg" alt="Instance diagram of the magnet recycling study: pre-treatment and electrolysis activities connected through exchanges, with the FEEDS chain link carrying the 6.45 kg conversion, an electricity exchange pointing to the canonical Electricity material and its background dataset, variant edges, and provenance edges to the source paper." loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption>
      Study 34 in the graph: two unit processes, their boundary exchanges, the chain
      link, the canonical-material join, and the scenario-variant edge — all from one
      supplementary table, all traceable.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>The test setup</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>The architecture is a read-only projection, not a migration.</strong> SQLite
    stays the system of record — the LLM extraction pipeline, the evaluation harness, and
    the web app never touch the graph. A mechanical exporter rebuilds a Neo4j projection
    from it, and if the hypothesis failed I'd delete the projection and nothing was lost:
  </p>
  <pre>papers / ERP ─► extraction pipeline ─► SQLite (system of record)
                                             │  exporter: 1:1, full rebuild
                                             ▼
                                       Neo4j 5 (Docker)
                                             │
                                             ▼
                              Cypher checklist + hand-check scripts</pre>
  <p>
    Two decisions made the exporter boring, which was the point. First: exchanges are
    first-class nodes (<strong>Exchange-as-node</strong>), so every row type in the
    relational schema has exactly one graph counterpart — the mapping is 1:1 and the
    exporter is ≈180 lines with no judgment calls. Second: <strong>full rebuild,
    always</strong> — no incremental sync, no migration logic, the
    projection is disposable by design. The engine is Neo4j 5 in Docker, the production
    target in the specs and the engine both grounding papers used; an embedded Cypher
    engine was the named fallback if infra friction appeared — the vocabulary and the
    queries wouldn't change.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The test was a six-question checklist run on real data</strong> — three
    papers on rare-earth magnet recycling, plus an industrial BoM (an ERP export for a
    polyester knit fabric, three hierarchy levels deep). Two disciplines kept it honest.
    Every answer that could be checked by hand, was: the graph's numbers were diffed
    against hand-calculated results computed independently from SQLite and the ERP
    spreadsheet. And the falsification clause was real: I wrote and ran SQL versions of
    the checklist side-by-side, because "SQL is equally easy" was a valid outcome.
  </p>
  <h3>Results</h3>
  <figure>
    <img src="/snapshots/lci-graph/bom-graph-projection.png" alt="Neo4j graph view of an industrial bill of materials: three assembly levels (orange), twelve leaf exchanges (yellow), and eleven canonical materials, connected by PART_OF, CONSUMES, and INSTANCE_OF edges." loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption>
      An industrial BoM living in the same graph as the paper world: three assembly levels,
      twelve leaf exchanges, eleven canonical materials. The disperse dye at the top
      is <em>one</em> node consumed by two process steps — in the spreadsheet it's two rows
      joined only by a human reading the article number.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    <strong>The vocabulary held (H1).</strong> Both worlds — academic LCIs and an
    ERP-sourced BoM — were represented with zero new node labels and zero new edge
    types, and every node traces back to its source: 100% provenance completeness.
    Suppliers rode along as edge properties, and the SAP article numbers gave identity
    links for free — no fuzzy matching in the ERP world at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The identity gap became enumerable (H2).</strong> One study's electricity
    was fragmented across seven process-specific material nodes — 28 exchanges — while
    the other two papers shared a single canonical electricity node. In the graph that
    gap is one query's worth of missing identity edges, closable by a single matching
    engine with no per-study special-casing; in the relational store it was invisible
    except by eye.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The roll-up math was checked by hand (H3).</strong> Collapsing a study to
    one black box — internal flows cancel, boundary flows sum, quantities rescale along
    the chain — matched a hand-calculated roll-up exactly, for all eight scenario
    variants of the study, including the variants where the chain's conversion
    denominator changes mid-chain. Writing the query surfaced a real design rule: a
    strict black-box must cancel <em>both</em> sides of an internal link, or the
    provider's reference product leaks into the boundary.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The honest nuance (H4).</strong> On flat lookups, the graph and plain SQL
    tied — near-identical line counts, same results, recorded honestly. The divergence
    showed up where hierarchy lives: flattening the BoM — "a lot of work" when done by
    hand for every downstream consumer — is one depth-agnostic traversal
    (<code>PART_OF*1..</code> with a running quantity product), reproducing the
    hand-flattened ERP export exactly, down to 1083.5188 kg of PET chips per tonne of
    fabric through two nested levels. The SQL equivalent is a recursive CTE carrying the
    same running product. So the graph earns its keep on composition, roll-ups, and
    provenance chains — and that nuance is load-bearing: the migration spec's acceptance
    criteria are anchored on those hierarchical queries, not on corpus-scale lookups
    where the graph can't justify itself.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The limits, recorded.</strong> One industrial BoM (n=1, textiles) with zero
    material overlap against the paper corpus — the cross-world join is machinery-tested,
    not data-tested. Multi-hop roll-up is untested, because every chain in the corpus is
    depth-1. And line-count is a weak proxy for effort. All three are written into the
    specs as open risks, not swept under the rug.
  </p>

  <h2>Where it stands</h2>
  <p>
    The gate verdict was <strong>GO</strong>: the hypothesis is confirmed — H4 in its
    nuanced form — and the migration specs were re-scoped on the evidence. The migration
    is now defined as the same full-rebuild exporter (the projection stays disposable,
    and match-decision history stays relational). Next is the unified matching engine —
    the machinery that closes the 28-exchange identity gap the PoC enumerated, and that
    will later join BoM materials to paper materials — then the production seam that
    lets the graph backend replace the relational one behind a flag.
  </p>
  <p class="muted-note">
    Screenshots are static snapshots of the local platform; there is no live demo —
    the database contains other people's published data, shown here for illustration.
  </p>
</Base>
